{"parentUuid":"38835d13-9791-4705-9574-6eeea7035e9f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFc19EAELTwHoNFY1Hw8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0169KyPGZhs81Rf7XNR5k4NR","name":"ExitPlanMode","input":{"plan":"# Plan: Fine-tune a house-method + agent model on the code + LONGVIEW corpus\n\n## Context\n\nGoal: a local model that has internalized not just *the code* but *how we got there* — the\nreasoning, decisions, and working method behind Prime-Silo / S16 / memo-ray / LONGVIEW — so it can\nsupport development, investigations, and task execution/management.\n\nThe instinct is \"train it on everything.\" The honest engineering split is:\n\n- **Facts** (\"why RocksDB over Neo4j\", \"the min_candidates lesson\") — a **retrieval** problem.\n  Fine-tuning stores facts unreliably (blurs, hallucinates). You already have strong fact infra:\n  S16 binary store (RocksDB+USearch), memo-ray, LONGVIEW KG, Bridge graphs. **RAG owns facts.**\n- **Method + voice + agent behavior** (verify-before-commit, dry-run-first, additive design,\n  ADR/card house style, tool-use patterns) — this is what **QLoRA fine-tuning is genuinely good at.**\n\nSo the target is a **hybrid**: QLoRA distills *how we work* and *how we drive tools*; RAG (existing\nstores) supplies *facts* at inference. We are **not** doing from-scratch pretraining, and **not**\ncramming facts into weights.\n\nUser-chosen scope: **Goal 1 (house method + voice)** and **Goal 3 (agent / task execution)**.\nPure factual recall stays a RAG job and is not a fine-tuning target.\n\n## Hardware roles (confirmed)\n\n- **Trainer (self-contained)** — Lenovo T480 (i5-8th gen, 16GB sys RAM) + **Razer Core X** eGPU over\n  Thunderbolt-3 + **Sapphire Pulse RX 9060 XT, 16GB VRAM** (RDNA4 / **gfx1200**). Runs the QLoRA jobs\n  **and** carries a full clone of the Benny home (see below), so it builds data, trains, and serves\n  RAG locally — no hard LAN dependency during training.\n- **Source-of-truth / workspace** — this desktop (Windows 11, `OneDrive\\binary16`). Holds the\n  canonical corpus (prime-silo, s16, memo-ray, LONGVIEW cards/KG). The clone is taken from here.\n- **Serving + agent tier (portable)** — **Benny** (multi-endpoint router, LAN-repoint proven at\n  `192.168.68.125:1234`). Benny is **not** a trainer — it serves the tuned model (LM Studio/lemonade\n  endpoint) and is the harness the model drives for Goal 3. Runs on either box.\n\n### Transfer strategy — clone the Benny home\nRather than split data desktop↔trainer over LAN, **clone the entire `PRIME_SILO_HOME` (the \"Benny\nhome\")** to the T480 so the trainer is self-contained. This home already carries, behind the existing\nNode+Python home resolver: the code, the RAG stores (S16 RocksDB+USearch, memo-ray data), LONGVIEW\ncards/KG, and the Benny runtime. Re-point `PRIME_SILO_HOME` on the trainer and everything resolves.\n\n**Gotcha to verify (Phase 0.5):** the clone crosses **Windows → Ubuntu**. RocksDB/USearch/memo-ray\nfiles are x86-64 same-endian and expected portable, but this must be **proven, not assumed** — open\neach store on Linux and read a known key before trusting it. The clone is a **snapshot**; treat it\nlike the vendored-fork drift lesson — record the source commit/timestamp and re-clone (or delta-sync)\nto refresh, don't let it silently rot.\n\n### What the AMD/Unsloth support actually is (constraints)\n- **OS decision: the T480 stays on Windows.** Unsloth documents a **native-Windows ROCm install**\n  (with runtime compatibility patches) plus WSL2. So Windows is a real path — but RDNA4 on Windows\n  ROCm is the newest surface. Primary path: native-Windows ROCm; fallback: WSL2 ROCm.\n- RX 9060 XT (gfx1200) **is** on Unsloth's supported list. Method: **QLoRA 4-bit / LoRA** (no\n  from-scratch pretrain). Requires **ROCm 6.0+**; RDNA4 realistically wants **ROCm 6.4.2+**.\n- Bonus of staying Windows: the Benny-home clone is **Windows→Windows**, so the store-portability\n  risk disappears and Benny (zero-install bundle targets Windows-x64) runs natively on both boxes.\n- 16GB VRAM → comfortable QLoRA on a **7–8B** model; 12–14B marginal. TB3 eGPU bandwidth is fine\n  for QLoRA (model stays resident in VRAM; per-step transfer is small).\n\n## Top risk (de-risk first, before any data work)\n**RDNA4 (gfx1200) in a TB3 eGPU under native-Windows ROCm** is the least-proven link in AMD's stack.\nThe GPU must enumerate (`hipInfo`/`rocm-smi`) and Unsloth's Windows runtime patches must load. Phase 0\nexists solely to prove a tiny real QLoRA step runs end-to-end on this exact hardware before investing\nin the data pipeline. If native-Windows ROCm can't be made stable, fallbacks in order:\n**(1) WSL2 ROCm** on the same Windows install (no reformat); **(2)** rent a cloud ROCm/CUDA box for\n*training only* and keep serving local via Benny; **(3)** smaller base model. Windows stays the host\nin all three.\n\n---\n\n## Phase 0 — Prove the trainer (hardware de-risk), Windows host\n1. On the T480 (Windows): install AMD ROCm-on-Windows (6.4.2+ for RDNA4) + the AMD Adrenalin/eGPU\n   driver. Confirm the Razer Core X + 9060 XT is seen by Windows and survives TB3 hot-plug/reboot.\n2. Verify the GPU enumerates for compute: `rocm-smi` / `hipInfo` show **gfx1200**, 16GB.\n3. Install Unsloth per its **native-Windows ROCm** guide (auto-installs ROCm PyTorch + bitsandbytes +\n   Triton + Windows runtime patches). If native install won't detect the GPU, switch to the **WSL2\n   ROCm** fallback before spending more time.\n4. **Smoke test:** QLoRA on a 7B (e.g. Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct) for ~30 steps on a toy dataset.\n   Confirm: it starts, VRAM tracking works, loss goes down, adapter saves, merged GGUF loads.\n\n**Gate:** a real QLoRA step completes on the 9060 XT under Windows (or WSL2). Do not proceed until\ngreen. **Verify, don't assume** — capture `rocm-smi` output + a loss curve as evidence. This is the\nmake-or-break gate; if neither Windows path works, move training to cloud and keep everything else.\n\n## Phase 0.5 — Clone the Benny home to the trainer (Windows→Windows)\n1. On the desktop, snapshot `PRIME_SILO_HOME` (code + RAG stores + LONGVIEW cards/KG + Benny runtime).\n   Record source commit/timestamp so the clone's provenance is known.\n2. Transfer to the T480 (external SSD / robocopy). Set `PRIME_SILO_HOME` on the trainer to the clone.\n   Same-OS, so RocksDB/USearch/memo-ray files carry over as-is — no format conversion.\n3. Sanity-check locally: Benny boots against the clone, the Node+Python home resolver points at it,\n   and a known key reads back from each store (S16 doc+vector, a memo-ray session, LONGVIEW cards).\n   Watch for absolute-path assumptions baked into the home (different drive letter / user dir).\n\n**Gate:** trainer boots Benny from the clone and every store reads locally; home resolver green.\n\n## Phase 1 — Data pipeline (the actual moat)\nFine-tune quality is ~90% data. The moat is that the corpus is *already structured*: LONGVIEW cards,\nADRs, session synthesis, tool-use traces. Convert it to instruction/preference data. With the cloned\nhome in place this can run **on the trainer itself** (or on the desktop — same code, same corpus).\n\nReuse existing infra rather than re-deriving:\n- LONGVIEW card/graph pipeline: `prime-silo/scripts/longview/longview.mjs` + `lib/` (`store.mjs`,\n  `walk.mjs`, `card_triples.mjs`, `retrieve.mjs`, `record.mjs`, `leak_gate.mjs`).\n- memo-ray store (already ingests Claude/Antigravity session + tool-use traces).\n- **Privacy: run `lib/leak_gate.mjs` over every emitted training row** — job-application / CV /\n  personal context must never enter the corpus (established leak-gate discipline; per operator profile).\n\nTwo data streams (one shared builder, two emitters):\n- **Stream A — method + voice (Goal 1), SFT.** From LONGVIEW cards + ADRs + session synthesis →\n  `(instruction → house-style response)` pairs. Response style = measured claims, dry-run-first,\n  additive framing, ADR/card structure. Target a few thousand high-quality pairs, not volume.\n- **Stream A' — preference pairs (Goal 1), DPO (optional, phase 4).** `verify-before-commit`\n  corrections are natural (rejected = first attempt, chosen = corrected). Perfect preference data.\n- **Stream B — agent trajectories (Goal 3), SFT.** From Benny / offload-orchestrator / Claude session\n  tool-use traces → `(state + goal → next tool call)` sequences. Teaches *how you drive tools*, not\n  facts.\n\nDeliverables: `scripts/train/build_dataset.mjs` (emits JSONL for A + B), a **held-out eval set**\ncarved out *before* training, and a small hand-audited **gold set** (~200 rows) eyeballed for quality.\n\n**Gate:** dataset built, leak-gate clean, gold set reads like *us*. Dry-run the builder first.\n\n## Phase 2 — First QLoRA run + honest eval\n1. Base: **Qwen2.5-Coder-7B-Instruct** (or Qwen3-8B) — continuity with the existing qwen stack.\n2. QLoRA (rank 16, lr 2e-4, packed 16k ctx) on Stream A + B combined. Track loss/VRAM.\n3. **Define success before training.** Eval on the held-out set: base vs tuned on house-method tasks\n   and tool-call correctness. RAG stays *separate* so we measure the fine-tune's own contribution.\n4. Merge adapter → GGUF for serving.\n\n**Gate:** tuned model measurably beats base on the held-out method/agent tasks. Report the delta\nplainly (no \"trained on how we got there\" claims without the number behind it).\n\n## Phase 3 — Wire into the stack (additive, gated)\n1. Serve the merged GGUF via LM Studio/lemonade; point **Benny's multi-endpoint router** at it.\n   With the cloned home this can be **fully local on the trainer** (model + RAG stores both local),\n   or served to the desktop over LAN via the proven repoint pattern — same router config either way.\n2. Register the tuned model as a **candidate engine behind the existing router** — additive, does\n   **not** replace the current engine. RAG (S16/LONGVIEW) stays the fact source at inference.\n3. For Goal 3, let it drive the **offload orchestrator (ADR-004)** on real dev tasks behind the\n   existing green/yellow/red gate. Keep the honest ledger/digest discipline.\n\n**Gate:** tuned engine handles a real task through Benny + offload gate, with RAG grounding, no\nregression vs the current engine.\n\n## Phase 4 — Scale (optional, later)\n- DPO on Stream A' preference pairs (verify-before-commit corrections).\n- Larger base or longer context if 16GB allows; refresh dataset from new sessions (delta mode).\n\n---\n\n## Verification (end-to-end)\n- **Phase 0:** `rocm-smi`/`hipInfo` show gfx1200 on Windows; a 30-step QLoRA completes; GGUF loads.\n- **Phase 0.5:** Benny boots on the trainer from the clone; S16/memo-ray/LONGVIEW stores read locally;\n  `PRIME_SILO_HOME` resolver green (no stale absolute paths).\n- **Phase 1:** builder dry-run emits JSONL; `leak_gate.mjs` reports 0 leaks; gold-set spot-check.\n- **Phase 2:** held-out eval table (base vs tuned) for method tasks + tool-call accuracy.\n- **Phase 3:** Benny router serves the tuned model; one real task runs through the offload gate with\n  RAG grounding; side-by-side vs current engine shows no regression.\n\n## Open decisions (can be resolved during Phase 0/1)\n- Native-Windows ROCm vs WSL2-ROCm on the T480 (decide from whichever detects the eGPU in Phase 0;\n  Windows stays the host either way).\n- Base model: Qwen2.5-Coder-7B vs Qwen3-8B (pick from the Phase-0 smoke test + eval fit).\n","planFilePath":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\.claude\\plans\\mellow-tinkering-swan.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":520,"cache_read_input_tokens":85349,"output_tokens":395,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":520,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_availabl